From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 16:21:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: qcom: Leave the controller alone in suspend when the EP
 powers off

On the Google Pixel 2 XL (msm8998, "qcom,pcie-msm8996"), the Easel
coprocessor (Intel 8086:3140) behind the controller's single root port
powers itself off the bus during the first system sleep. Its power
sequencing is owned by the device's own firmware and PMIC; the kernel
neither causes this nor can undo it by retraining the link.

The suspend path then wedges the SoC deterministically on the second
s2idle cycle:

  - cycle 1 suspend: link up, nothing to do. During the sleep the
    endpoint drops off the bus. Cycle 1 resume: pcie->suspended is
    false, nothing to do. The controller is now powered with a dead
    link.
  - cycle 2 suspend: dw_pcie_link_up() reports link down, so
    qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq() runs qcom_pcie_host_deinit().
  - cycle 2 resume: qcom_pcie_host_init() runs against the absent link
    partner and wedges the interconnect - sometimes inside the
    controller's own resume callback, sometimes at the root port's
    first config access right after. The DPM watchdog fires and the
    device resets.

RAM-persistent breadcrumb instrumentation attributed five out of five
such hangs to the dpm_resume_noirq phase, in the controller's device
(1c00000.pcie) or the root port (0000:00:00.0). Gating only the
host_deinit()/host_init() pair was not enough: two residual hangs were
attributed to the remainder of the resume callback, whose
qcom_pcie_icc_opp_update() performs a link-status DBI read via
dw_pcie_link_up() - on this platform even that access hangs the noirq
phase once the partner is gone. The only configuration measured stable
is the controller left completely untouched across the sleep cycle,
exactly as it is left after the (always successful) first cycle: 85
consecutive s2idle cycles with zero failures, versus a historical
ceiling of one clean cycle pair, with the endpoint enumerated and WLAN
associated throughout.

When the root port carries the endpoint-powered-off-in-suspend
property, skip both noirq callbacks entirely: keep the controller
powered across sleep and do not touch the link, the DBI space, or the
interconnect votes. The cost is the idle power of an inert controller
kept on across sleep, on boards that opt in via DT.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c | 29 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
index c48a20602d7f..82713eb19f9d 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -285,6 +285,7 @@ struct qcom_pcie {
 	struct list_head ports;
 	bool suspended;
 	bool use_pm_opp;
+	bool ep_powered_off_in_suspend;
 };
 
 #define to_qcom_pcie(x)		dev_get_drvdata((x)->dev)
@@ -1925,6 +1926,15 @@ static int qcom_pcie_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 			goto err_pm_runtime_put;
 	}
 
+	for_each_available_child_of_node_scoped(dev->of_node, of_port) {
+		if (of_node_is_type(of_port, "pci") &&
+		    of_property_read_bool(of_port,
+					  "endpoint-powered-off-in-suspend")) {
+			pcie->ep_powered_off_in_suspend = true;
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, pcie);
 
 	irq = platform_get_irq_byname_optional(pdev, "global");
@@ -1988,6 +1998,21 @@ static int qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq(struct device *dev)
 	if (!pcie)
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * If the endpoint is powered off in system suspend by an agent the
+	 * kernel does not control, the link is dead or dying and neither
+	 * end of it can be safely operated on. Taking the link-down branch
+	 * below powers the controller off, and the host reinitialization
+	 * this queues up for resume then runs against an absent link
+	 * partner, which wedges the SoC interconnect on msm8998. Even the
+	 * bare link-status DBI read in qcom_pcie_icc_opp_update() hangs the
+	 * noirq resume phase in this state. Leave the controller fully
+	 * powered and untouched across the sleep cycle: the one
+	 * configuration measured stable on such boards.
+	 */
+	if (pcie->ep_powered_off_in_suspend)
+		return 0;
+
 	/*
 	 * Set minimum bandwidth required to keep data path functional during
 	 * suspend.
@@ -2048,6 +2073,10 @@ static int qcom_pcie_resume_noirq(struct device *dev)
 	if (!pcie)
 		return 0;
 
+	/* The controller was left untouched by qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq(). */
+	if (pcie->ep_powered_off_in_suspend)
+		return 0;
+
 	if (pm_suspend_target_state != PM_SUSPEND_MEM) {
 		ret = icc_enable(pcie->icc_cpu);
 		if (ret) {
